WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.254
"Diet and Caloric Restriction for Longevity—
The Monkey Trials"

00:00:07.254 --> 00:00:10.260
Calorie restriction
in primates to extend lifespan.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.520
Will it work?

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.460
There's only one way
to find out.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.380
There have been
four investigations

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.680
of calorie restriction and
lifespan in nonhuman primates.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.800
The first was published in 2003,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.760
an analysis of the mortality
of 117 rhesus monkeys

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.620
followed for about 25 years
in a lab,

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.640
eight of whom had their
Purina monkey chow restricted.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.580
The average survival
of the restricted monkeys

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.920
was to 32 years of age,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.520
compared to 25 years
for the control monkeys.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.460
However, it was more of
an observational study,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.040
since the monkeys weren't
randomly assigned,

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.360
and although in the abstract

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.220
they talk about
the survival advantage

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.000
and how the ad libitum monkeys,
the eat-all-you-want monkeys,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.100
had more than twice
the risk of death,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.560
they acknowledge
deeper in the paper

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.880
that the difference in death

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.000
did not reach
statistical significance,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.580
meaning it may very well
have been a fluke.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.400
That was all we had, though,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.080
until results
started trickling in

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.260
from the famous pair of studies
that involved randomizing

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.960
about 200 rhesus monkeys
to caloric restriction

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.060
or more normal diets,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.340
one out of the University
of Wisconsin-Madison

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.100
and another from the
National Institute of Aging.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.240
The UW study reported a 30
percent caloric restriction

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.820
significantly delayed disease
and improved survival,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.660
but the NIA study did not.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.000
In the UW study,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.220
caloric restriction
reduced the incidence

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.440
of age-related diseases
including cancer,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.620
cardiovascular disease,
brain atrophy,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:44.820
and muscle wasting,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.500
preserving muscle mass,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.620
demonstrating caloric
restriction can delay aging,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.540
but in the NIA study,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.280
the difference
in age-related diseases

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.460
did not achieve
statistical significance.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.900
In the UW study,

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.440
the restricted monkeys lived
to an average of about 29 years

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.940
compared to the control monkeys,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.760
who lived closer to the average
for monkeys in captivity

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.260
of about 26 years old.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.800
In contrast, though one
of the restricted monkeys

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.400
in the NIA study became
the longevity record holder

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.080
for the species at age 43,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.520
on average, the restricted group
didn't live any longer

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.960
than the control animals.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.860
Why the disparate results
between the two studies?

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.900
In the NIA study that found no
significant lifespan difference,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.960
the control group
was not fed ad libitum,

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.720
but rather had food
portioned out to prevent

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:37.980
excessive weight gain.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.140
In contrast,
in the UW study,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.360
the control monkeys could eat
as much as they wanted

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.140
throughout the day

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:47.880
so ended up weighing more
than the NIA control animals.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.060
Of course caloric restriction
would improve

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.640
the health and survival
of overweight monkeys

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.680
just as weight loss
would be beneficial

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:56.880
for overweight people,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.300
but the null results
in the NIA study suggest

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.160
that normal weight people
might not benefit

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.900
from restricting further.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.220
The NIA monkeys were also fed
a healthier diet.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.200
The diet in the UW study

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.780
where they saw significant
benefit to cutting down

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.020
was an ultra-processed
concoction of largely

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.900
milk protein, corn oil,
corn starch, and table sugar,

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.100
whereas the NIA diet actually
included unprocessed plant foods

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.020
such as corn,
soybeans, and wheat,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.300
and so actually had
some phytonutrients.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.920
About 29 percent of the UW diet
was straight sugar,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.740
compared to 4 percent
in the NIA diet.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.880
American adults get about
17 teaspoons of sugar a day,

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.500
which is about
13 percent of calories.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.940
The contrasting findings suggest
that the worse your diet is,

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.180
the more important it is
to eat less of it.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.660
The fourth study
was on grey mouse lemurs,

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.400
among the smallest of primates,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.440
standing just three inches tall.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.800
Those randomized to
a 30 percent caloric restriction

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.780
compared to an ad libitum
control group

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.020
lived a whopping
50 percent longer.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.740
And not just average lifespan,
but maximal lifespan.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.460
The maximum lifespan was boosted
by about 20 percent.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.760
However,
the "calorie restricted" group

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.720
still weighed heavier
than their wild counterparts,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.420
so again this may just be
an illustration

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.860
of the harms of obesity

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.460
and another indictment against
all-you-can-eat buffets.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.300
Also, those in the calorie
restriction group experienced

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.600
an acceleration of age-related
loss of grey matter

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:34.740
throughout their brains,

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.680
though this did not appear
to translate into cognitive

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:38.940
or behavioral differences.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.780
Pooling the three rhesus monkey
studies together,

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.740
there seemed to be
lower age-related mortality

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.700
but no significant difference
in average lifespans overall

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.740
between the caloric
restriction groups

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.120
and the control groups.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.040
Given the time and expense,

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.080
there is little chance
we are going to see any more

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.520
long-term primate studies

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.560
on caloric restriction
for life extension.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.100
So what can we draw
from the primate data to date?

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.580
If you're overweight
or living off junk food,

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.220
eating less is a good idea.

